@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@
+# Demand forecasting module - implementation notes
+
+Nothing here was run: there is no PHP/MySQL in the working environment, so the code has been written and read
+through but not executed. The first thing to do on a test installation is to run update 44 on a copy of an existing
+database and to install a fresh one, then walk through the pages.
+
+## What was built
+
+A **Forecasting** module (module link `forecast`, report link `fc`) placed right after Manufacturing in the main menu,
+with ten pages and a shared function library.
+
+| Page | Page security | Purpose |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ForecastDashboard.php` | 2 | Active forecast / summary counts, converted MRP demands, history range, links to everything |
+| `ForecastSalesHistory.php` | 11 | Extract the sales history (range of months, re-running replaces the range) |
+| `ForecastGenerate.php` | 11 | Item or whole-category forecasts, 12 methods or best fit, batch progress and result list |
+| `ForecastReview.php` | 11 | Search forecasts, period by period calculated / revised / last year / variance, enter or clear revisions |
+| `ForecastSummary.php` | 11 | Summary forecasts by stock category, customer type, sales area, salesperson + review of the items behind each |
+| `ForecastAccuracy.php` | 2 | MAD, POA, MSE, RMSE per item, category and method for a month range; low accuracy flagged |
+| `ForecastMethodComparison.php` | 2 | One item, all methods side by side on a hold-out with their accuracy |
+| `ForecastParameters.php` | 15 (administrator) | Company defaults: alpha, beta, gamma, season length, MA periods, hold-out months, default horizon (plus the extras below) |
+| `ForecastToMRP.php` | 11 | Convert active forecasts to MRP demands (replace / incremental) |
+| `ForecastInquiry.php` | 2 | View forecasts by item, category, location and month |
+
+Page security reuses existing tokens: 2 (inquiries), 11 (the level of stock/item maintenance), 15 (administrator). No
+new security tokens were needed, so existing role/token assignments are untouched. The Forecasting menu also links
+the existing Master Schedule (`MRPDemands.php`), MRP Calculation (`MRP.php`) and MRP report (`MRPReport.php`); those
+pages keep their own security levels.
+
+Shared code: `includes/ForecastFunctions.php` (parameters, history access, the 12 methods, hold-out/best fit,
+metrics, storing forecasts).
+
+### Tables (all new, prefix `fc`)
+
+* `fcparameters` - company defaults (name/value). Also holds `historyfrom`/`historyto`, the range last extracted.
+* `fcsaleshistory` - monthly net quantity per item, location, month, customer type, sales area and salesperson.
+* `fcforecasts` / `fcforecastdetails` - header (item, location or `ALL`, method, parameters used, best-fit flag,
+  months of history, `active`, who/when) and one row per month with `calculatedqty` and nullable `revisedqty`.
+* `fcsummaryforecasts` / `fcsummarydetails` - the same for summary forecasts (dimension + value).
+* `fcmrpdemands` - the `mrpdemands` rows the converter wrote (see below).
+
+Plus one row in `mrpdemandtypes`: `FCST` "Forecast".
+
+## Where the sales history comes from
+
+`stockmoves` rows of type 10 (sales invoice, including counter sales) and 11 (credit note, including counter returns)
+with `hidemovt = 0`, by calendar month of `trandate`, per `stockid` and `loccode`; net quantity = `-SUM(qty)` (invoices
+are negative movements, credits positive). That is the same movement data the stock usage and sales inquiries are built
+on, not open sales orders. Each row is also tagged with the customer's type (`debtorsmaster.typeid`) and the branch's
+area and salesperson (`custbranch`) so summary forecasts can be built from the very same table (the item/location
+queries just add these up). Other stock-usage movement types (adjustments, work order issues) are deliberately not in.
+Re-extracting a range deletes the rows of those months first, in one transaction, so nothing is doubled.
+
+## How a forecast reaches MRP
+
+1. Each item/location has at most one **active** forecast; generating again sets the old one inactive (kept, with its
+   method and parameters, because the accuracy report compares past forecasts with what sold afterwards).
+2. `ForecastToMRP.php` takes the active forecasts for a month range and writes one `mrpdemands` row per item and
+   month under demand type `FCST`, quantity = revised quantity if entered, else calculated quantity, due on the 1st of
+   the month (today for the current month). Zero quantities are not written.
+3. Every row written is recorded in `fcmrpdemands` (demand id, item, month, forecast). **Replace** mode deletes only
+   recorded demands of the items in scope and horizon, then writes new ones; **incremental** skips item/months
+   already recorded. So converting twice never doubles, and demands entered by hand - even under type `FCST` - or by
+   other processes are never touched. Recorded rows whose demand was deleted in the master schedule are purged first.
+4. The normal MRP run (`MRP.php`) with "use MRP demands" then reads them from `mrpdemands` like any master schedule
+   entry. MRP itself was not modified.
+
+Revised quantity always wins: `COALESCE(revisedqty, calculatedqty)` is used by the review screen, inquiry, accuracy
+statistics and the MRP conversion; clearing the field stores NULL and restores the calculated quantity.
+
+## Database changes - new and existing installations
+
+* New installs: `install/sql/tables/fc*.sql` (picked up by the installer's directory scan), seed data in
+  `install/sql/data/` (`fcparameters.sql`, `mrpdemandtypes.sql`, `forecast_scripts.sql`, `forecast_menuitems.sql`;
+  `modules.sql` rewritten with Forecasting at sequence 7 and the later modules moved up one). `install/sql/demo.sql`
+  gets the same rows as statements. The installer already writes the highest update number into `DBUpdateNumber`.
+* Existing databases: `sql/updates/44.php` (creates tables, defaults, demand type, `scripts` rows, module for every
+  security role, menu items). It is safe to re-run.
+* **Existing users keep exactly their modules.** `www_users.modulesallowed` is a positional list (one 1/0 per module in
+  menu order), so adding a module in the middle would silently shift every later entry (a user with General Ledger
+  would get Forecasting and lose Asset Manager...). Update 44 inserts a `0` at the new position in every existing
+  user's list, so nobody gets Forecasting automatically; the administrator switches it on per user in User
+  Maintenance (`WWW_Users.php`, which now lists the module). The installer's admin user gets it. Customer logins
+  (`CustLoginSetup.php`) get a matching extra `0`.
+* Translations: all texts use `__()`. The new msgids were appended to `locale/en_GB.utf8/LC_MESSAGES/messages.pot`
+  (module, menu captions, page titles, method names, main messages). A few minor strings (table headings and the like)
+  are not in the template yet; the usual "Rebuild default language file" will pick them up. Menu captions are stored in
+  English in `menuitems` and translated on display, like the existing menu.
+
+## Methods
+
+1 percent over last year (p from parameters), 2 growth from the last two years (last 12 / previous 12 months), 3 same
+month last year, 4 moving average of N, 5 line through two points (mean of the first 3 and last 3 of the most recent
+12 months), 6 least squares over all history, 7 quadratic regression (normal equations), 8 flexible (weights on the
+prior months, most recent first, applied recursively to forecast months), 9 weighted moving average (weights normalised
+to 1), 10 simple exponential smoothing, 11 Holt, 12 Holt-Winters (additive seasonality). All results are rounded to
+2 decimals and floored at 0. Minimum histories are exactly the BRD's; a shorter series is reported ("N months of
+history, the method needs at least M") and not forecast.
+
+Best fit: the last *hold-out months* (default 3) are hidden, every applicable method forecasts them, lowest MAD wins
+(ties: lower method number). For a category all items are tested first (in batch steps), then the method applicable to
+the most items with the lowest total absolute error relative to total hold-out sales is used for all of them.
+
+## BRD ambiguities and how they were resolved
+
+* **History length of an item.** A series starts at the item's first month with sales inside the held range; earlier
+  months are not padded with zeros (that would be made-up history). Zeros after that are real months without sales.
+* **Partial months.** Extraction only accepts ranges ending at the last complete month, and a new range must overlap
+  or touch the held one (otherwise the gap would read as zero sales).
+* **"Active item" for category runs:** not discontinued and not a dummy/service item (`mbflag <> 'D'`).
+* **Location.** MRP demands have no location, so for MRP: an item's active "all locations" forecast is used if there
+  is one, otherwise its location forecasts are added - never both. Stock location user restrictions
+  (`locationusers`) are not applied to forecasting pages.
+* **Which forecast is compared in accuracy** when an item was forecast repeatedly: for each item/location/month the
+  latest forecast generated before that month started, using revised quantities. POA = total forecast / total actual x
+  100; "low accuracy" = more than the *accuracy tolerance* (default 20 points) from 100, or a forecast with no sales.
+* **Summary forecasts** are not revisable and do not go to MRP (MRP needs items); the "detail behind a summary" is
+  the list of contributing items with their share of the last 12 months. Category summaries use the item's current
+  category.
+* **Linear approximation / flexible method** are under-specified in the BRD; the two-point and weight definitions
+  above are my interpretation. **Holt-Winters** is additive.
+* **Extra parameters** beyond the BRD list (all in `fcparameters`, editable): percent over last year, flexible and
+  weighted-MA weights, accuracy tolerance, variance highlight (default 25%), batch size (items per step).
+* **Batch generation** runs in steps of *batch size* items kept in the session, with a progress bar that auto-continues
+  (button fallback) and a cancel button; forecasts of finished steps stay if cancelled.
+* **Dates in the UI** use month inputs (YYYY-MM) rather than the system date format, because everything is monthly.
+* **Page security level 11 vs 9:** MRP itself is level 9, but 9 is also what supplier logins carry, so the new pages
+  use 2/11/15 instead.
+* `fcsaleshistory` is not wired into the "change stock code / delete sales" Z_ utilities.
